# The First Shadow

In the beginning, when the world was still soft and new, there lived a young weaver named Kira who worked only by sunlight. She created tapestries of such beauty that even the birds would pause mid-flight to admire them.

One day, the Sun fell deeply in love with Kira, watching her work from dawn until dusk. But the Sun grew jealous of the night, when Kira would rest and he could not see her. In his longing, he decided he would never set again.

Days passed without darkness. The world grew hot and parched. Plants withered, animals sought shelter, and Kira could not rest. Exhausted, she wept.

The Moon, seeing Kira's suffering, approached the Sun. "Your love is blinding you," she said gently. "True devotion means letting go."

The Sun finally understood. As he set that evening, his last rays touched Kira, and from her body stretched the world's first shadow—a dark silhouette that would stay with her always.

Now the Sun could watch over Kira even when absent, through the shadow he'd gifted her. And that is why every living thing casts a shadow: a reminder that love means both presence and release.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

